  {
    question: "Is managed IT support worth it for a business with 5 staff?",
    answer:
      "Often, yes, but a lighter plan may fit better. At five staff you may not need a premium all-inclusive agreement; a co-managed or essentials plan covering security, backups and helpdesk can run a few hundred dollars a month total. The real question is what an outage or a cyber incident would cost you, not what support costs.",
  },
  {
    question: "Why do MSP quotes for the same business vary so much?",
    answer:
      "Because 'managed IT' isn't a standard product. One quote might include security tooling, backup licensing and on-site visits; another might list them all as extras. Always compare what's included line by line. The cheapest headline price is frequently the most expensive agreement once the extras land.",
  },
  {
    question: "Do managed IT providers charge GST on top of quoted prices?",
    answer:
      "Quotes to businesses are commonly shown ex-GST in Australia, but practices vary. Always confirm whether a quoted per-user price includes GST before comparing providers.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can I just pay hourly instead of a monthly fee?",
    answer:
      "You can. It's called break-fix, and at typical rates of $120–$200+ per hour it feels cheaper until something serious happens. Break-fix providers are also paid more when you have more problems, which is exactly the wrong incentive. For any business that genuinely depends on its systems, fixed-fee managed support almost always works out cheaper over a year.",
  },
  {
    question: "What should a 10-person business expect to pay in total?",
    answer:
      "As a rough planning figure, a 10-person Australian business should expect somewhere around $1,000–$2,500 per month for fully managed IT, depending on inclusions, security requirements and how complex the environment is. Get fixed quotes in writing and compare inclusions, not just totals.",
